  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Find the coordinates of $A$ which is the point of intersection of the curve $y=\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2$ and the line $y=4.$\n\nThe coordinates of $A$ are $($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2 \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3x-2}+2 & =4 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3x-2} & =2 \\\\ 5 & =6x-4 \\\\ 9 & =6x \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we get $y=4.$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $A=(\\frac{3}{2},4).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066764/assets/f07fc53462af6fbb7de0a901ae23721a2eb84f6e83bd5577a2659c175519fc67.png)",
